  "name": "Clearing Rational Expression in Linear Inequalities",
  "problem": "Now, let's solve an inequality that contains a rational expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2y-1}{3} \\lt 5\n$$\n\nFirst, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y-1}{3} & <5 \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{2y-1}{3}) & <3\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{2y-1}{\\cancel{3}}) & <15 \\\\ 2y-1 & <15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-1 & <15 \\\\ 2y-1+1 & <15+1 \\\\ 2y & <16 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{2} & <\\frac{16}{2} \\\\ y & <8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y <  8.$",
